<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Prussian Uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1260–1274</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Prussian Uprising was the largest revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, which had conquered them in the mid-13th century. Encouraged by the Order's defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> at Durbe in 1260, the Prussian tribes rose under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herkus-monte">Herkus Monte</a> of the Natangians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwanus">Diwanus</a> of the Bartians and besieged the Order's castles. Reinforced by crusaders, the knights crushed it by 1274, and Prussian resistance never recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising.jpg" alt="Great Prussian Uprising" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baltic_Tribes_c_1200.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
